  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThrough Monday: Continued dry through the period with cooler than \nnormal temperatures tonight and in the northeast Monday but, \ntemperatures will warm back above normal in the southwest Monday \nafternoon. Mid afternoon satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the \ncirculation around a positively tilted trough over our region. This \ntrough will continue shifting east through Monday while mid level \nridging strengthens back over our region from the west. High \npressure at the surface centered to the northwest this afternoon \nwill shift east as well through the period but, continue to ridge \nsouth across our CWA. Our PWAT got knocked down below an inch and a \nquarter and with continued northerly low level flow through Monday \nour airmass will remain rather dry. This will help mitigate any heat \nstress concerns due to the lower relative humidity Monday afternoon. \n/22/\n\nTuesday through next Saturday: Overall, the weather through mid \nweek in our forecast area will be mostly unimpactful given there \nwill be a continued lull in the heat danger, and less than the \nusual convective rainfall coverage. The trough mentioned above \nwill gradually shift east of the area Tue-Wed, and northerly low \nlevel flow behind it will help to keep the boundary layer drier \nthan we've observed recently, thus keeping heat danger at more \ntolerable levels.\n\nUnfortunately, the guidance consensus is for the Bermuda high to \nexpand westward across the southeast CONUS late this week, and \nthis will lead to heat rebuilding over our area. Some positives to\ntake away in the upcoming pattern are that low level flow will \nhave more of an onshore/southerly component, and the strength of \nthe ridge will not be all that impressive. This means that while \nthe dangerous heat will return, it is not expected to reach the \nextreme levels experienced recently (i.e., it will be more of a \nheat advisory scenario vs an extreme heat warning). Otherwise, \nincreasing anticyclonic flow will continue to help limit diurnal \nconvective coverage. /EC/",
